course: caro-kann
name: Caro-Kann Defence
color: black
eco: B10 B11 B12 B13 B14 B15 B18 B19
tagline: Rock-solid against 1.e4, with a light-squared bishop that never gets stuck.
description: 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5. Black supports d5 with the c-pawn, gets the c8 bishop out before ...e6, and reaches healthy structures with good endgames. This course covers the Advance, Classical, Exchange, Panov, Two Knights, Fantasy and the 2.c4 and early-deviation lines.

chapter: advance
title: Advance Variation: 3.e5
intro: White grabs space with e5. You develop the bishop to f5 before ...e6, then hit White's centre with ...c5. Against the aggressive Nc3 and g4 lines, the bishop drops back to g6 and you strike with ...c5 at once.

line: adv-short
name: Short System: Nf3 and Be2
moves: 1.e4 c6 {Prepare ...d5 with the c-pawn, keeping the c8 bishop's diagonal open.} 2.d4 d5 {Challenge e4 straight away.} 3.e5 Bf5 {The key Caro-Kann move: develop the bishop before ...e6 shuts it in.} 4.Nf3 e6 5.Be2 c5 {Hit the base of White's pawn chain.} 6.Be3 Nd7 {Support c5 and keep an eye on e5.} 7.O-O Ne7 {Heading for c6 or f5, piling up on d4.} 8.Nbd2 h6 {Give the bishop a retreat on h7 against Nh4 ideas.} 9.c3 Nc6 {Comfortable and solid. Plans: ...Qb6 and ...cxd4 against White's centre.}

line: adv-c3
name: 4.c3 solid setup
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.e5 Bf5 4.c3 e6 5.Be2 c5 {Hit the chain early.} 6.Nf3 Nc6 7.O-O cxd4 {Release the tension when White's pieces are committed.} 8.cxd4 Nge7 9.Nc3 Nc8 {The knight heads for b6, eyeing c4.}

line: adv-bd3
name: 4.Bd3: trade bishops
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.e5 Bf5 4.Bd3 Bxd3 {Happy to trade: White's best bishop is gone.} 5.Qxd3 e6 6.Nf3 Qb6 {Pressure on d4 and b2.} 7.O-O c5 8.c3 Nc6 9.dxc5 Bxc5 {Excellent development and no bad bishop.}

line: adv-nc3
name: 4.Nc3 e6 5.g4: the sharp line
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.e5 Bf5 4.Nc3 e6 5.g4 {White chases the bishop.} Bg6 6.Nge2 c5 {Counterattack in the centre straight away: the best answer to a flank attack.} 7.Be3 Nc6 8.dxc5 Nxe5 {Black wins the e5 pawn back.} 9.Nd4 Nc6 {Black is solid and the g4 push has left holes.}

line: adv-h4
name: 4.h4: grabbing space
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.e5 Bf5 4.h4 h5 {Stop h4-h5, which would trap your bishop.} 5.c4 e6 6.Nc3 Ne7 7.Nge2 dxc4 {Take the pawn when White's bishop is still on f1.} 8.Ng3 Bg6 9.Bxc4 Nd5 {The d5 outpost is yours.}

line: adv-short-castle
name: Short System: 6.O-O
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.e5 Bf5 4.Nf3 e6 5.Be2 c5 6.O-O {Castling first.} Nc6 7.c3 cxd4 8.cxd4 Nge7 {Heading for f5 or c8-b6.} 9.Nc3 Nc8 {The knight reroutes to b6 to watch c4 and d5.}

line: two-nf3-advance
name: 2.Nf3 d5 3.e5
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.Nf3 d5 3.e5 Bf5 {Same idea as the Advance.} 4.d4 e6 {Back in the Short System.}

chapter: classical
title: Classical: 3.Nc3 dxe4 4.Nxe4 Bf5
intro: White plays 3.Nc3. You take on e4 and develop the bishop with tempo on the knight. In the main line, White chases the bishop with Ng3 and h4, you meet h4 with ...h6 and trade bishops, then you castle and play ...c5 or ...e5.

line: classical-main
name: Main line with h4-h5
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.Nc3 dxe4 {Take: White's knight comes to e4, where you can hit it.} 4.Nxe4 Bf5 {Develop the bishop with tempo.} 5.Ng3 Bg6 6.h4 h6 {Don't allow h5 trapping the bishop.} 7.Nf3 Nd7 {Stop Ne5 from hitting the g6 bishop.} 8.h5 Bh7 9.Bd3 Bxd3 {Trade the bishops: your structure becomes very solid.} 10.Qxd3 e6 11.Bd2 Ngf6 12.O-O-O Be7 13.Kb1 O-O {A super-solid Caro-Kann. Plans: ...c5 or ...Qc7 and ...Rad8.}

line: classical-nf3
name: 6.Nf3 without h4
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.Nc3 dxe4 4.Nxe4 Bf5 5.Ng3 Bg6 6.Nf3 Nd7 {Control e5.} 7.Bd3 Bxd3 {Trade the bishops.} 8.Qxd3 e6 9.O-O Ngf6 10.Bf4 Be7 11.Rfe1 O-O {Comfortable equality: ...c5 next.}

line: classical-nc5
name: 5.Nc5
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.Nc3 dxe4 4.Nxe4 Bf5 5.Nc5 {Hits b7.} e6 {If 6.Nxb7?, 6...Qb6 traps the knight.} 6.Bd3 {A natural move, but the bishop now blocks the queen's defence of d4.} Qxd4 {Win a clean pawn.} 7.Nb3 Bb4+ 8.Bd2 Qe5+ {Development with check. You're a pawn up.}

line: classical-bd3
name: 5.Bd3: trade on e4
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.Nc3 dxe4 4.Nxe4 Bf5 5.Bd3 {White offers a pawn for development.} Qxd4 {Take the pawn: it's sound.} 6.Nf3 Qd8 {Back home safely. You're a pawn up.} 7.Qe2 Bxe4 8.Bxe4 Nf6 9.Bd3 e6 {White has some development but no real compensation.}

line: classical-nd2
name: 3.Nd2 move order
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.Nd2 dxe4 4.Nxe4 Bf5 {Same as 3.Nc3.} 5.Ng3 Bg6 6.h4 h6 7.Nf3 Nd7 {Main-line Classical.}

line: classical-h5-early
name: 7.h5 straight away
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.Nc3 dxe4 4.Nxe4 Bf5 5.Ng3 Bg6 6.h4 h6 7.h5 {White grabs space first.} Bh7 8.Nf3 Nd7 9.Bd3 Bxd3 10.Qxd3 e6 {Same structure as the main line.}

chapter: exchange
title: Exchange and Panov: 3.exd5
intro: After 3.exd5 cxd5 White either plays the quiet Exchange (Bd3, c3) or the Panov (c4), aiming for an isolated pawn position. In the Exchange you develop actively with ...Nc6 and ...Bg4. In the Panov you play ...Nf6 and ...e6 and blockade the d-pawn.

line: exchange-main
name: Exchange: Bd3 and c3
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.exd5 cxd5 {Recapture with the pawn: a symmetrical, solid structure.} 4.Bd3 Nc6 5.c3 Nf6 6.Bf4 Bg4 {Active bishop: pins the f3 knight once it arrives.} 7.Qb3 {Hits b7.} Qd7 {Defends b7 calmly.} 8.Nd2 e6 9.Ngf3 Bxf3 10.Nxf3 Bd6 {Trade off White's best bishop.} 11.Bxd6 Qxd6 12.O-O O-O {Equal. Plan: the minority attack with ...Rab8 and ...b5.}

line: panov
name: Panov: 4.c4
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.exd5 cxd5 4.c4 {White goes for an isolated d-pawn.} Nf6 5.Nc3 e6 6.Nf3 Be7 7.cxd5 Nxd5 {Blockade d5.} 8.Bd3 Nc6 9.O-O O-O 10.Re1 Nf6 {Classic IQP position: trade pieces and the d4 pawn becomes weak.}

line: panov-bg5
name: Panov: 6.Bg5
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.exd5 cxd5 4.c4 Nf6 5.Nc3 e6 6.Bg5 Be7 7.Nf3 O-O 8.Rc1 Nc6 {Pressure on d4.} 9.c5 h6 10.Bf4 Ne4 {Strike in the centre. Black is fine.}

line: panov-c5
name: Panov: 7.c5
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.exd5 cxd5 4.c4 Nf6 5.Nc3 e6 6.Nf3 Be7 7.c5 {White grabs queenside space.} O-O 8.Bd3 b6 {Undermine c5 straight away.} 9.b4 a5 {Strike back: the queenside opens for your pieces.}

chapter: sidelines
title: Two Knights, Fantasy and other tries
intro: White can avoid the main lines with 2.Nc3 and 3.Nf3 (Two Knights), 3.f3 (Fantasy), 2.c4, or a slow 2.d3. Each has one simple antidote.

line: two-knights
name: Two Knights: 2.Nc3 and 3.Nf3
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.Nc3 d5 3.Nf3 Bg4 {Pin the knight: the bishop gets out early, as always.} 4.h3 Bxf3 5.Qxf3 e6 {A solid French-like structure with no bad bishop.} 6.d4 Nf6 7.Bd3 dxe4 8.Nxe4 Qxd4 {Win a pawn.} 9.c3 Qd8 {Back to safety. Black is a pawn up and solid.}

line: fantasy
name: Fantasy: 3.f3
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.d4 d5 3.f3 {Supports e4 at the cost of weakening the kingside.} e6 {Calm and solid: prepare ...c5 and ...Bb4.} 4.Nc3 Bb4 {Pin the knight, pressuring e4.} 5.Bf4 Nf6 6.e5 Nfd7 7.a3 Bxc3+ 8.bxc3 c5 {Hit the centre. Black's position is excellent.}

line: c4
name: 2.c4: Accelerated Panov
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.c4 d5 {Strike the centre at once.} 3.exd5 cxd5 4.cxd5 Nf6 {Win the pawn back later.} 5.Nc3 Nxd5 6.Nf3 Nc6 7.Bb5 e6 8.O-O Be7 {Comfortable development for Black.}

line: kia
name: 2.d3: slow setups
moves: 1.e4 c6 2.d3 d5 3.Nd2 e5 {Grab the centre with both pawns.} 4.Ngf3 Bd6 5.g3 Nf6 6.Bg2 O-O 7.O-O Re8 {A great version of the Caro-Kann for Black.}

quiz: classical-main 12
q: Why play 6...h6 here?
a: So h5 doesn't trap the g6 bishop
w: To prepare ...g5 and attack
w: To stop Bg5 forever
why: After h4-h5 the bishop would have no squares. With ...h6 played, the bishop can drop back to h7.

quiz: adv-short 6
q: White played 3.e5. What's the key Caro-Kann idea now?
a: 3...Bf5: develop the bishop before ...e6 locks it in
w: 3...e6 straight away
w: 3...Bg4 pinning nothing
why: The whole point of the Caro-Kann: the light-squared bishop gets outside the pawn chain, unlike the French.

quiz: classical-nc5 10
q: Black played 5...e6. Why not worry about 6.Nxb7?
a: 6...Qb6 traps the knight on b7
w: The knight is protected after Nxb7
w: Because b7 is defended by the bishop
why: After 6.Nxb7 Qb6 the knight has no squares: 7.Nc5 Bxc5 8.dxc5 Qxc5 and Black wins material back with interest.

quiz: adv-nc3 12
q: White chased your bishop with g4. What's the principled reply?
a: Strike the centre with ...c5
w: Play ...h5 and attack g4
w: Retreat ...Bc8 and regroup
why: A flank attack is best met with a counterattack in the centre. ...c5 hits d4 while White's pieces are pointed at the kingside.
